1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,073
Money, art, weapons, lives.

2
00:00:03,074 --> 00:00:14,000
These are the targets that tempt thieves, inspiring ever more sophisticated means of protection.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Security Systems, now on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:50,369 --> 00:00:55,160
Mankind uses methods both primitive and complex to protect what he holds dear.

5
00:00:55,161 --> 00:01:02,360
Today, 21st century technology safeguards our most precious possessions

6
00:01:02,361 --> 00:01:07,240
Night vision cameras and infrared sensors seek out intruders

7
00:01:07,241 --> 00:01:13,700
Computers measure the minute details of fingers, eyes and faces

8
00:01:13,701 --> 00:01:16,860
To ensure positive identification

9
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,460
Three-dimensional holograms verify the authenticity of valuables

10
00:01:23,461 --> 00:01:26,220
from currency to fine cigars.

11
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:32,980
Invisible electronic tags identify and track valuable works of art.

12
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:38,820
But even the most sophisticated machines

13
00:01:38,821 --> 00:01:42,620
rely on the skill and honesty of their human masters.

14
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:47,480
For any security system, it always comes down to people.

15
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:49,280
Whether it's a physical security system,

16
00:01:49,380 --> 00:01:51,160
whether it's a computer security system,

17
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:53,140
it always comes down to people.

18
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:57,120
You've got to have people you can trust who will do the right thing.

19
00:01:58,340 --> 00:02:01,400
Since the earliest days of human civilization,

20
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:05,080
there have been those who sought to rob their fellow man.

21
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:11,080
Until recently, thicker walls and better locks were the most effective means of protection.

22
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:18,300
The first serious vaults were developed in ancient Egypt,

23
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:22,280
where the wealthy few ease their passage into the next life

24
00:02:22,281 --> 00:02:25,540
by filling burial chambers with fantastic treasures.

25
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:29,400
The lure of unimaginable riches

26
00:02:29,401 --> 00:02:32,700
drew a constant procession of grave robbers

27
00:02:32,701 --> 00:02:36,580
who persisted despite the risk of severe punishment.

28
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:39,480
Some had their noses chopped off.

29
00:02:39,481 --> 00:02:44,480
Others were banished to the desert to face an excruciating death.

30
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:50,940
By 2600 BC, Pharaoh Cheops had grown tired

31
00:02:50,941 --> 00:02:54,180
of seeing his ancestors' tombs ransacked and looted.

32
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:57,260
Determined to avoid their fate,

33
00:02:57,720 --> 00:03:00,180
he ordered construction of the Great Pyramid

34
00:03:00,181 --> 00:03:02,080
to house his burial vault.

35
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:06,060
This towering 13-acre monument

36
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:07,860
remains the largest structure

37
00:03:07,861 --> 00:03:10,460
to ever guard one man's wealth.

38
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:18,220
At times, up to 100,000 laborers toiled

39
00:03:18,221 --> 00:03:22,480
to place two and a quarter million blocks of limestone and granite.

40
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:27,660
A labyrinth of interior tunnels lead to the king's tomb

41
00:03:27,661 --> 00:03:29,680
and to many decoy chambers.

42
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:32,000
When the pharaoh was interred,

43
00:03:32,320 --> 00:03:36,000
workmen released three gigantic plugs that blocked the corridors.

44
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:38,660
When the last priest emerged,

45
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,440
he closed a single stone slab on a pivot.

46
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:44,720
The pyramid was perfectly symmetrical,

47
00:03:44,721 --> 00:03:49,900
making it impossible to detect the entrance door from among the identical stones.

48
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:55,880
For 400 years, the crypt held its secret

49
00:03:55,881 --> 00:04:01,740
until finally an unknown band of marauders pierced the defenses

50
00:04:01,741 --> 00:04:04,440
and stole the treasures within.

51
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:11,700
In Cheops' day, very few people possessed anything worth stealing.

52
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:23,040
But as cities grew and commerce increased, early banks arose to safeguard mankind's newfound affluence.

53
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:30,860
From Athens to Jerusalem, moneylenders depended on the local gods to deter would-be thieves.

54
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:38,600
Before there were security systems, before there were policemen, where money was collected by moneylenders, they operated out of temples.

55
00:04:38,601 --> 00:04:41,260
They actually used superstition as a security system.

56
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:47,340
Thieves would fear to go into the sanctuary and bring down the wrath of God for actually committing a theft in a temple.

57
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:49,380
And it actually worked quite well for a long time.

58
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:58,840
Beginning in 2000 BC, the Egyptians pioneered the first mechanical locks to protect their homes.

59
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:06,680
They devised large wooden keys with a series of pegs that matched holes drilled into the doorpost.

60
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:17,960
These eventually evolved into heavy steel padlocks and later into the more sophisticated tumbler lock.

61
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:25,020
Each cut of the key corresponds to an individual lever tumbler inside the lock

62
00:05:25,021 --> 00:05:29,420
and has to lift that individual tumbler the way that you're seeing the front one on the screen.

63
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:33,900
Unlocked and locked.

64
00:05:33,901 --> 00:05:41,100
Although the tumbler lock has periodically been modified and improved,

65
00:05:41,101 --> 00:05:45,860
its basic design still enjoys widespread use.

66
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:53,780
In the Middle Ages, keys became status symbols

67
00:05:53,781 --> 00:05:59,120
as wealthy landlords with great mansions employed a keeper of the keys

68
00:05:59,121 --> 00:06:01,680
to carry bulky keychains.

69
00:06:01,681 --> 00:06:05,420
locks became increasingly ornate

70
00:06:05,421 --> 00:06:09,260
serving double duty as protection and decor

71
00:06:09,261 --> 00:06:15,480
clever locksmiths devised trick mechanisms for large chests

72
00:06:15,481 --> 00:06:18,000
that traditionally held valuables

73
00:06:18,001 --> 00:06:24,240
when the crook would reach in there and put his fingers in those holes to lift out this tray

74
00:06:24,241 --> 00:06:28,520
it would set off a steel trap which could either grab his fingers

75
00:06:28,521 --> 00:06:31,840
and keep him trapped right there at the site until the owner arrives

76
00:06:31,841 --> 00:06:36,580
or be so painful that he'll cry out and alert anyone to come and find him

77
00:06:36,581 --> 00:06:38,480
or indeed cut his fingers off.

78
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:45,120
By the 18th century, more and more people were entrusting their money and valuables to banks,

79
00:06:46,100 --> 00:06:50,360
prompting ambitious thieves to turn from housebreaking to bank robbery.

80
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:57,680
Early safes were simply strong boxes that could be carried away and broken into at leisure.

81
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:04,520
As a result, manufacturers began embedding vaults into the bank building.

82
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:15,720
The 19th century saw a rising tide of bank robberies and the speedy improvement of bank vaults.

83
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:23,180
The California gold rush of 1849 inspired and then crushed many dreams of wealth.

84
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:29,280
For failed miners, robbing gold was a way of sharing the good fortune of others.

85
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:39,440
Later, during the Civil War, the James Gang raided gold from Yankee banks to buy Confederate guns.

86
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:47,200
When the war ended, you have these trained, riding, violent bank robbers

87
00:07:47,201 --> 00:07:50,000
who are now in a dispossessed country, especially the Southerners.

88
00:07:50,001 --> 00:07:51,300
and see, they have a choice.

89
00:07:51,380 --> 00:07:53,780
They can get a job hauling hay for the Yankees

90
00:07:53,781 --> 00:07:56,900
or they can get on their horse and get their six-shooter and go rob banks.

91
00:07:58,160 --> 00:08:00,960
Banks responded by constructing vaults

92
00:08:00,961 --> 00:08:04,480
able to withstand the most powerful explosive of the day,

93
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:06,200
nitroglycerin.

94
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:11,980
These safes had walls with several inches of steel on either side

95
00:08:11,981 --> 00:08:14,840
and up to several feet of concrete between the steel.

96
00:08:14,841 --> 00:08:22,020
Unable to crack safes by force, robbers turned to terror.

97
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:28,380
They dragged bank presidents to their vaults, forcing them at gunpoint to open the combination locks.

98
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:31,180
I'll give you everything that belongs to me, but that money isn't mine.

99
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:35,760
If you don't want anything to happen to that kid of yours, open it up.

100
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:41,260
Bank executives pleaded for a solution that would protect them from the threat of kidnapping.

101
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:49,160
For 4,000 years, man had sought a thief-proof lock.

102
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:55,200
In 1873, New York inventor James Sargent made the dream come true.

103
00:08:55,820 --> 00:09:01,860
He developed the Time Lock, an ingenious device still used in most large vaults today.

104
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:05,220
The mechanism sits inside the door and out of reach.

105
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:09,840
At the designated time, cogs in the timepiece pull back a bar,

106
00:09:09,841 --> 00:09:13,640
freeing levers so bolts can be opened by the right combination.

107
00:09:14,840 --> 00:09:22,180
No force and no man can open the vault until the appointed hour.

108
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:24,080
You, open that safe.

109
00:09:29,340 --> 00:09:30,560
Go on, open it.

110
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:33,940
Sorry, but it's not a time lock.

111
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:35,900
A time lock?

112
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:39,440
That's right.

113
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:41,360
It won't open until 9.30.

114
00:09:42,620 --> 00:09:44,120
You've got a 12-minute wait.

115
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:52,140
To set the time, you use a regular key in this keyhole.

116
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:56,420
That will allow us to unlock and open the cover, revealing the mechanism.

117
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:02,820
And then we set the number of hours that we want the vault to stay locked by pressing these various pins.

118
00:10:05,020 --> 00:10:08,540
Time locks allowed bank presidents to sleep soundly.

119
00:10:08,541 --> 00:10:15,660
With vaults all but impenetrable, thieves turned to daring daylight heists backed by blazing guns.

120
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:27,180
The exploits of Dillinger, baby-faced Nelson, and Bonnie and Clyde thrilled a depression-weary nation.

121
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:34,420
But behind the screaming headlines was a reality of brief lives and violent death.

122
00:10:34,421 --> 00:10:41,180
John Dillinger, public enemy number one, died a killer's death in this alley beneath a hail of police bullets.

123
00:10:49,180 --> 00:10:56,060
The invulnerability of modern bank vaults was dramatically confirmed in the last days of World War II.

124
00:10:56,061 --> 00:11:08,720
In Hiroshima, an American-made safe stood in the Teoku Bank just 900 feet from ground zero.

125
00:11:09,860 --> 00:11:16,180
The atomic blast incinerated the bank and everything in the vicinity, but the vault survived.

126
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:20,660
Later, the bank would be rebuilt around it.

127
00:11:20,661 --> 00:11:27,560
With banks secure, modern day robbers have turned to more vulnerable targets

128
00:11:27,561 --> 00:11:30,080
Many try to burglarize homes

129
00:11:30,081 --> 00:11:37,400
This in turn has led millions to safeguard their houses with an array of motion detectors

130
00:11:37,401 --> 00:11:43,200
Any breach triggers an alert at the local police station

131
00:11:43,201 --> 00:11:46,020
and at a centralized command headquarters

132
00:11:46,021 --> 00:11:59,740
whereas before some companies had major alarm centers in every city in the country now most

133
00:11:59,741 --> 00:12:03,620
of the major companies only have two or three throughout the whole country and it's just like

134
00:12:03,621 --> 00:12:08,180
when you call for an airline reservation you dial that 800 number you might be in Philadelphia but

135
00:12:08,181 --> 00:12:14,620
that person may be in LA but even the most sophisticated security system is vulnerable

136
00:12:14,621 --> 00:12:15,940
to human error.

137
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:19,420
Twenty percent of homeowners leave their alarms

138
00:12:19,421 --> 00:12:22,540
on the default setting of 1-2-3-4,

139
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:25,680
and millions more use codes or combinations

140
00:12:25,681 --> 00:12:28,320
decipherable by clever thieves.

141
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:33,760
Very often, people will pick a number that they can remember.

142
00:12:33,940 --> 00:12:34,860
That's only natural.

143
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:38,700
The thing is, if you pick your birthday or your anniversary

144
00:12:38,701 --> 00:12:41,100
or the last four digits of your phone number

145
00:12:41,101 --> 00:12:44,200
or other digits that are very significant

146
00:12:44,201 --> 00:12:45,660
that are easy for you to remember,

147
00:12:46,160 --> 00:12:48,540
they're also easy for the crook to figure out

148
00:12:48,541 --> 00:12:50,320
if the crook does a little homework on you.

149
00:12:50,920 --> 00:12:54,640
So the best advice when you're picking any kind of a combination or PIN

150
00:12:54,641 --> 00:12:58,020
is to come up with a number that's easy to remember

151
00:12:58,021 --> 00:12:59,620
but that is not related to you.

152
00:12:59,900 --> 00:13:03,260
Make it a friend's telephone number or a friend's address, not yours.

153
00:13:05,780 --> 00:13:09,900
Money can be hidden and secured behind thick steel walls.

154
00:13:09,901 --> 00:13:13,940
But when we return, the battle to protect very public

155
00:13:13,941 --> 00:13:15,080
works of art.

156
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,280
When President Franklin Roosevelt

157
00:13:20,281 --> 00:13:22,320
forgot the combination of his safe,

158
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:24,080
an expert correctly

159
00:13:24,081 --> 00:13:27,300
guessed that it was 34-33.

160
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:29,340
A variation

161
00:13:29,341 --> 00:13:31,600
on the date of his first inaugural.

162
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:35,640
Security systems will continue

163
00:13:35,641 --> 00:13:38,040
in a moment on Modern Mars.

164
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:43,580
Down through the centuries,

165
00:13:43,581 --> 00:13:46,600
great artworks have cast a powerful spell.

166
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:49,520
Men would travel hundreds of miles

167
00:13:49,521 --> 00:13:52,480
to spend a day in the sublime presence

168
00:13:52,481 --> 00:13:54,020
of the Venus de Milo.

169
00:13:55,660 --> 00:13:56,700
Since the Vikings,

170
00:13:57,160 --> 00:13:59,140
victorious armies have plundered art

171
00:13:59,141 --> 00:14:00,440
from conquered lands.

172
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:03,740
Caesar's Roman Empire absorbed masterpieces

173
00:14:03,741 --> 00:14:05,280
from each defeated foe.

174
00:14:07,060 --> 00:14:09,940
Napoleon brought the treasures of Egypt and Italy

175
00:14:09,941 --> 00:14:11,240
back home to France,

176
00:14:11,241 --> 00:14:14,760
where Hitler would later pilfer them yet again.

177
00:14:17,160 --> 00:14:19,580
When the Germans entered France during World War II,

178
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:22,480
Hermann Goering starts sacking the museums of France,

179
00:14:22,880 --> 00:14:26,540
which had presumably been filled by the efforts of Napoleon and other kings of France,

180
00:14:26,740 --> 00:14:29,980
so that the artwork starts becoming almost a trophy of empire.

181
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:35,160
Goering went to elaborate lengths to protect his plunder

182
00:14:35,161 --> 00:14:39,080
from both the ravages of nature and from enemy soldiers.

183
00:14:39,081 --> 00:14:44,320
Much of the loot was hidden in the labyrinthine tunnels of a German salt mine.

184
00:14:45,220 --> 00:14:48,340
Pure air with steady humidity flowed through the tunnels.

185
00:14:49,060 --> 00:14:52,200
The very conditions that would preserve paintings for years.

186
00:14:53,300 --> 00:14:55,800
And, much like the Great Pyramid of Egypt,

187
00:14:56,440 --> 00:15:01,540
the mine contained many bricked-up chambers designed to confuse potential robbers.

188
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:06,180
As Allied forces approached Germany in 1945,

189
00:15:06,181 --> 00:15:09,920
Goering tried to remove all signs of the buried treasure

190
00:15:09,921 --> 00:15:12,440
by ordering the cave entrances blown up.

191
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:17,160
The security might well have worked

192
00:15:17,161 --> 00:15:20,980
but for German informers who tipped off Allied authorities

193
00:15:20,981 --> 00:15:24,840
American soldiers uncovered the artwork

194
00:15:24,841 --> 00:15:28,720
and one enterprising GI shipped the loot back home to Texas

195
00:15:28,721 --> 00:15:32,640
and there it would stay until the 1970s

196
00:15:32,641 --> 00:15:34,780
when it was finally returned to Europe.

197
00:15:36,180 --> 00:15:44,920
The most famous art theft in history has its roots in the fertile imagination of Leonardo da Vinci.

198
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:52,460
When Leonardo was dying, he gave the Mona Lisa to Francis I.

199
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:55,220
He gave the painting to the French king.

200
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:59,220
And it was not considered at the time such a great deal.

201
00:15:59,380 --> 00:16:02,120
I think Francis hung it in his bathroom.

202
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:06,020
But soon the world grew to love the Mona Lisa.

203
00:16:06,180 --> 00:16:15,380
She was moved to the Louvre Museum in Paris, where the only security system was the eyes and ears of the guards.

204
00:16:17,080 --> 00:16:24,040
Yet art theft was so rare that when the painting disappeared in 1911, no one suspected the worst.

205
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:30,500
For more than 24 hours, on the spot on the wall where the Mona Lisa should be grinning out at the people coming to the museum,

206
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:33,780
there was a blank space. There was a hook with nothing there.

207
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:35,220
No one questioned this.

208
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:36,480
None of the guards questioned this,

209
00:16:36,560 --> 00:16:38,440
and none of the management or the people who ran the museum

210
00:16:38,441 --> 00:16:39,820
questioned this for 27 hours

211
00:16:39,821 --> 00:16:43,340
because paintings were regularly being taken from their spots for photography

212
00:16:43,341 --> 00:16:44,680
or to be cleaned off.

213
00:16:45,060 --> 00:16:47,380
And in this case, they just assumed that's what had happened to the Mona Lisa.

214
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:53,360
Finally, museum officials realized that the unthinkable was true.

215
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:56,160
The Mona Lisa had been stolen.

216
00:16:57,040 --> 00:16:58,420
For the next two years,

217
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:02,440
French authorities would search in vain for their national treasure.

218
00:17:02,441 --> 00:17:05,420
then in 1913

219
00:17:05,421 --> 00:17:07,320
Vincenzo Perugia

220
00:17:07,321 --> 00:17:08,760
was arrested in Florence

221
00:17:08,761 --> 00:17:10,680
with the painting in his possession

222
00:17:10,681 --> 00:17:13,380
and a strange tale to tell

223
00:17:13,381 --> 00:17:17,540
Perugia and two accomplices

224
00:17:17,541 --> 00:17:19,380
had hidden in a storage room

225
00:17:19,381 --> 00:17:20,600
the night before the theft

226
00:17:20,601 --> 00:17:22,380
at dawn

227
00:17:22,381 --> 00:17:24,620
the three men simply changed

228
00:17:24,621 --> 00:17:25,900
into the white tunics

229
00:17:25,901 --> 00:17:28,040
worn by Louvre cleaning crews

230
00:17:28,041 --> 00:17:30,480
removed the painting from the wall

231
00:17:30,481 --> 00:17:33,840
and carried it into the streets of Paris.

232
00:17:36,440 --> 00:17:40,500
But they were just the front men for the mastermind behind the theft.

233
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:46,620
He had conned six American buyers into paying $300,000 apiece

234
00:17:46,621 --> 00:17:48,300
for the real Mona Lisa

235
00:17:48,301 --> 00:17:51,820
and then arranged for six expert forgeries.

236
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:54,340
Once news of the theft broke,

237
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:58,300
he sold each of the six copies as the original masterpiece.

238
00:17:59,300 --> 00:18:01,300
His plan, it's fascinating.

239
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:06,400
You buy a painting, you think it's a real Mona Lisa that's been stolen for you.

240
00:18:07,060 --> 00:18:10,900
Even if you find out you've been cheated, who are you going to go to?

241
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:16,060
You know, you're going to go to the police and say, he told me it was a real one.

242
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:18,740
It was foolproof.

243
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:23,060
It was absolutely 100% no fail.

244
00:18:23,061 --> 00:18:29,340
In 1913, the Mona Lisa returned triumphantly to the Louvre.

245
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:31,880
A bigger celebrity than ever.

246
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:35,700
And a warning that art everywhere was vulnerable.

247
00:18:36,900 --> 00:18:39,840
When the painting toured the United States in 1963,

248
00:18:40,480 --> 00:18:42,440
it was treated like a visiting hero,

249
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:48,140
protected from adoring crowds by a long motorcade and 100 guards.

250
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:52,240
Its special traveling crate had bulletproof glass

251
00:18:52,241 --> 00:18:55,120
and maintained constant temperature and humidity.

252
00:18:56,320 --> 00:18:59,980
Even President Kennedy turned out to welcome the honored guest.

253
00:19:01,940 --> 00:19:07,600
This painting is the second lady that the people of France have sent to the United States.

254
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:12,220
And though she will not stay with us as long as the Statue of Liberty,

255
00:19:13,320 --> 00:19:15,240
our appreciation is equally great.

256
00:19:16,320 --> 00:19:19,320
Five million people a year view the Mona Lisa,

257
00:19:19,321 --> 00:19:24,820
and the Louvre has taken extensive measures to protect its most famous resident.

258
00:19:25,680 --> 00:19:30,660
Today it is completely covered over, completely closed in,

259
00:19:31,280 --> 00:19:35,160
roofed over, temperature controlled, humidity controlled,

260
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:41,200
guards right, left and center, bulletproof glass in front.

261
00:19:42,020 --> 00:19:46,240
In my opinion, it is no longer a work of art.

262
00:19:46,340 --> 00:19:47,320
It is an icon.

263
00:19:47,321 --> 00:19:55,060
In 1961, the British government paid 140,000 pounds to buy Goya's famed portrait,

264
00:19:55,620 --> 00:19:58,180
the Duke of Wellington, from a private collector.

265
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:01,320
Less than a month later, it vanished.

266
00:20:03,860 --> 00:20:07,800
This great portrait of Wellington by Goya was esconced in the National Gallery,

267
00:20:07,940 --> 00:20:13,940
protected by infrared sensors, 24-hour guards, in the middle of the capital of Europe.

268
00:20:13,941 --> 00:20:15,360
It's the home of Scotland Yard.

269
00:20:15,361 --> 00:20:24,758
So when somebody was intrepid enough to go into the museum and steal this painting overnight, the assumption was only a master thief, only the personification of a

270
00:20:24,759 --> 00:20:33,060
Cary Grant from the movies could move through this world, steal this painting so swiftly and move away so elegantly and leave not a single clue.

271
00:20:33,061 --> 00:20:37,380
For four years, the painting remained at large.

272
00:20:38,580 --> 00:20:44,700
Then, in 1965, an unemployed bookmaker's clerk named Kempton Bunton

273
00:20:44,701 --> 00:20:48,620
had a few too many pints and admitted his guilt.

274
00:20:49,620 --> 00:20:53,900
61 years old, with thick glasses and an even thicker belt line,

275
00:20:54,240 --> 00:20:56,620
encompassing his 250 pounds,

276
00:20:57,080 --> 00:21:01,580
Bunton proved to astonished detectives that he was indeed the culprit.

277
00:21:03,060 --> 00:21:11,808
Kempton Bunton, the most unlikely of art thieves, had merely cased the museum, learned from the guards that the sensors were turned off in the wee hours of the morning

278
00:21:11,809 --> 00:21:12,960
for the cleaning crew.

279
00:21:13,540 --> 00:21:16,820
He sabotaged a lock into the bathroom window to get into the museum.

280
00:21:16,821 --> 00:21:24,420
He stole a ladder from a nearby church, up the ladder, through the window, gets the Wellington, back to the bathroom, down the ladder, back to Birmingham.

281
00:21:24,421 --> 00:21:27,920
Buntin's motive was simple.

282
00:21:28,700 --> 00:21:33,340
Angered that his government would spend 140,000 pounds for a painting

283
00:21:33,341 --> 00:21:39,620
while making old-age pensioners like himself pay a license fee to watch television.

284
00:21:40,500 --> 00:21:44,860
In the aftermath of the theft, the National Gallery retrained its guards

285
00:21:44,861 --> 00:21:47,940
and added alarms to exterior windows.

286
00:21:47,941 --> 00:21:57,300
Today, international art theft is a billion-dollar business.

287
00:21:57,301 --> 00:22:07,440
In major museums and private collections, 24-hour systems detect rapid changes in infrared energy caused by the body heat of an intruder.

288
00:22:10,260 --> 00:22:16,420
Valuable artworks are pinned with invisible electronic tags that identify the piece,

289
00:22:16,421 --> 00:22:20,560
sound an alarm if moved, and act as a homing beacon.

290
00:22:22,220 --> 00:22:26,820
So-called curtain protectors create a magnetic field around each painting

291
00:22:26,821 --> 00:22:30,860
that activate alarms and cameras if the field is disturbed.

292
00:22:34,760 --> 00:22:41,340
Beyond even physical security, art and other valuables must be protected from forgeries and fakes.

293
00:22:41,341 --> 00:22:48,760
The latest technology to ensure authenticity is three-dimensional holographic imaging

294
00:22:48,761 --> 00:22:55,720
Holograms are virtually impossible to counterfeit and can be embossed on any surface

295
00:22:57,320 --> 00:23:04,160
The process begins in sealed laboratories completely isolated from light, dust and vibration

296
00:23:04,161 --> 00:23:14,140
There, finely tuned laser beams are split and reassembled to create holograms, pixel by pixel

297
00:23:14,141 --> 00:23:21,120
Australia, Finland and Singapore already use holograms on their major currency

298
00:23:21,121 --> 00:23:27,040
And the United States Bureau of Engraving and Printing is experimenting with the technology

299
00:23:27,041 --> 00:23:34,860
When we return, protecting America's capital city.

300
00:23:39,140 --> 00:23:42,380
For democratic nations like the United States,

301
00:23:42,780 --> 00:23:46,680
there is an ongoing tension between maintaining an open society

302
00:23:46,681 --> 00:23:49,520
and safeguarding public buildings and officials.

303
00:23:50,760 --> 00:23:53,760
Although the White House and Capitol belong to the people,

304
00:23:53,761 --> 00:23:57,880
they must constantly be protected against potential assailants.

305
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:06,100
From the early days of the Republic, Washington was highly vulnerable.

306
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:11,300
The War of 1812 nearly left the city in ashes.

307
00:24:13,040 --> 00:24:19,120
In retaliation for the Americans burning the city of York up in Canada, which became Toronto,

308
00:24:19,121 --> 00:24:24,600
the British set fire to Washington, D.C., including the Capitol and the White House.

309
00:24:25,340 --> 00:24:29,040
And if it hadn't been for the fact that it started raining,

310
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:32,240
there would have been complete destruction of those buildings.

311
00:24:32,380 --> 00:24:35,560
I always tell people that God was on our side in that instance.

312
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:41,500
The fire left black soot over the executive mansion.

313
00:24:42,160 --> 00:24:44,720
To remove its stain, the building was whitewashed

314
00:24:44,721 --> 00:24:48,100
and for the first time became known as the White House.

315
00:24:49,120 --> 00:24:54,740
Despite this near disaster, in the following decades, only a few troops guarded the president's home.

316
00:24:57,180 --> 00:25:03,700
With Andrew Jackson, when he was elected president, afterwards they had a big inaugural party at the White House,

317
00:25:03,780 --> 00:25:11,700
which basically anybody who wanted to come could, and they had a drunken brawl where they were jumping up and down over the couches and everything else.

318
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:15,980
Even during the Civil War, the White House was open to all.

319
00:25:15,981 --> 00:25:21,760
common citizens lined up outside Lincoln's office to meet with him

320
00:25:21,761 --> 00:25:28,000
John Wilkes Booth's diaries revealed that he had cased the White House grounds

321
00:25:28,001 --> 00:25:30,520
during an aborted plot to kidnap Lincoln

322
00:25:30,521 --> 00:25:33,760
when Booth murdered Lincoln at Ford's Theater

323
00:25:33,761 --> 00:25:38,180
the president's entire security team consisted of one soldier

324
00:25:38,181 --> 00:25:40,960
who was busy drinking across the street

325
00:25:40,961 --> 00:25:54,961
World War II finally brought serious security measures to Washington.

326
00:25:56,300 --> 00:25:59,840
Anti-aircraft guns were mounted atop key public buildings.

327
00:26:00,900 --> 00:26:04,460
Military planners urged that the White House be camouflaged.

328
00:26:04,461 --> 00:26:08,000
but President Roosevelt rejected the idea

329
00:26:08,001 --> 00:26:09,380
as bad for morale

330
00:26:09,381 --> 00:26:12,300
and unbefitting a democratic society

331
00:26:12,301 --> 00:26:13,280
civilization

332
00:26:13,281 --> 00:26:15,740
being in such danger

333
00:26:15,741 --> 00:26:17,440
at the capitol building

334
00:26:17,441 --> 00:26:20,460
even the war did little to increase security

335
00:26:20,461 --> 00:26:23,520
for many years capitol policemen

336
00:26:23,521 --> 00:26:25,860
were hired via a patronage system

337
00:26:25,861 --> 00:26:28,200
you could call your congressman

338
00:26:28,201 --> 00:26:31,220
and the next day be patrolling with a gun and a badge

339
00:26:31,221 --> 00:26:38,180
Then, in 1954, Puerto Rican nationalists stormed the galleries

340
00:26:38,181 --> 00:26:41,300
shooting and injuring five members of Congress

341
00:26:41,301 --> 00:26:46,240
Hospital and emergency units quickly mobilized to care for the five wounded congressmen

342
00:26:46,241 --> 00:26:48,600
cut down in a fantastic sudden shooting

343
00:26:48,601 --> 00:26:53,420
In the wake of the assault, stricter standards of hiring and training

344
00:26:53,421 --> 00:26:55,840
were implemented for Capitol Hill guards

345
00:26:55,841 --> 00:27:01,680
and objects that can easily hide weapons from cameras to umbrellas

346
00:27:01,681 --> 00:27:03,800
were banned from the visitor galleries.

347
00:27:06,140 --> 00:27:11,900
The 1950s also saw new security for America's founding documents.

348
00:27:13,040 --> 00:27:18,160
In December 1952, a full-dress parade complete with armored car

349
00:27:18,161 --> 00:27:22,080
escorted the Declaration of Independence, the Constitution,

350
00:27:22,081 --> 00:27:26,720
and the Bill of Rights from the Library of Congress to the National Archives.

351
00:27:27,820 --> 00:27:31,520
Since then, they've been exhibited behind bulletproof glass.

352
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:37,560
At night, they're lowered into a 50-ton concrete silo 20 feet below the ground.

353
00:27:38,900 --> 00:27:44,000
This unique vault is perhaps the safest compartment man has ever devised.

354
00:27:44,500 --> 00:27:48,260
It's atomic-proof, burglar-proof, waterproof,

355
00:27:48,261 --> 00:27:54,160
and could withstand the weight of the entire archives exhibition hall collapsing on top.

356
00:27:56,460 --> 00:28:04,280
Not until the terrorist acts of the 1980s and 90s did Washington fundamentally change its approach to security.

357
00:28:05,540 --> 00:28:12,160
For a visit from President Sadat of Egypt, airport-style metal detectors were installed in the capital.

358
00:28:12,161 --> 00:28:19,560
machines like these are calibrated to detect the mass of metal present in the smallest handgun

359
00:28:19,561 --> 00:28:27,860
when a gun passes through the sensor its metal disrupts a magnetic field and triggers an alert

360
00:28:27,861 --> 00:28:34,360
in the aftermath of the bombing of the federal building in oklahoma

361
00:28:34,361 --> 00:28:37,340
pennsylvania avenue was closed to traffic

362
00:28:37,341 --> 00:28:43,980
hydraulic barriers now rise to block unauthorized vehicles

363
00:28:43,981 --> 00:28:48,280
cement flower pots dot the washington landscape

364
00:28:48,281 --> 00:28:52,060
protecting the white house capital and fbi headquarters

365
00:28:52,061 --> 00:28:59,200
while the exact strength of the barriers is classified information

366
00:28:59,201 --> 00:29:03,500
they are clearly sturdy enough to block bomb carrying vans

367
00:29:03,501 --> 00:29:12,560
Some of the federal government's most stringent security

368
00:29:12,561 --> 00:29:17,420
is devoted to sites that house one of the most dangerous substances known to man

369
00:29:17,421 --> 00:29:23,200
The Lawrence Livermore Laboratory outside Oakland

370
00:29:23,201 --> 00:29:25,680
is home to both nuclear weapons schematics

371
00:29:25,681 --> 00:29:29,680
and the plutonium used to construct atomic bombs

372
00:29:29,681 --> 00:29:34,140
when you're talking about the protection of special nuclear material

373
00:29:34,141 --> 00:29:39,100
one of the things that differentiates it from the protection of anything else that you might see

374
00:29:39,101 --> 00:29:42,260
is that our officers are allowed to use deadly force

375
00:29:42,261 --> 00:29:48,240
in repelling any attempts to get to the nuclear material

376
00:29:48,241 --> 00:29:53,000
so by federal law they're able to basically shoot you

377
00:29:53,001 --> 00:29:55,040
if it came to that

378
00:29:55,041 --> 00:30:04,600
No foreign national, even from allies like Canada or England, are allowed on the Livermore grounds without a background check.

379
00:30:06,660 --> 00:30:17,800
Those who work with sensitive material will undergo an extensive FBI investigation that includes questioning acquaintances from the distant past and takes up to a year

380
00:30:17,801 --> 00:30:18,600
to complete.

381
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:22,840
You can step on that little black pad there and swipe that badge through the reader for me.

382
00:30:22,841 --> 00:30:25,000
So you need to make sure the chair is not on there?

383
00:30:25,480 --> 00:30:30,620
The physical security is multi-layered with guard towers overseeing double fences

384
00:30:30,621 --> 00:30:34,940
with sensors that show the exact point of any perimeter breach.

385
00:30:37,660 --> 00:30:42,920
As workers approach building 332, the plutonium holding area,

386
00:30:43,260 --> 00:30:45,520
they pass through a series of double gates.

387
00:30:47,140 --> 00:30:51,680
To gain access, they need the proper badge, personal PIN number,

388
00:30:51,681 --> 00:30:53,860
and hand-scan identification.

389
00:30:56,260 --> 00:31:00,000
Hidden scales that register each individual's weight

390
00:31:00,001 --> 00:31:04,180
provide an additional safeguard against unauthorized entry.

391
00:31:05,780 --> 00:31:11,080
If the weight that is registered is significantly different

392
00:31:11,081 --> 00:31:14,400
from that which is included in the information contained

393
00:31:14,401 --> 00:31:16,240
on the magnetic strip in your badge,

394
00:31:16,740 --> 00:31:18,280
then you're rejected for entry.

395
00:31:18,281 --> 00:31:21,740
You're not allowed to come into the security area.

396
00:31:22,400 --> 00:31:28,180
The reason for weighing you is to ensure that there's no one else in the booth with you,

397
00:31:28,500 --> 00:31:34,320
that you're not being coerced or no one is holding you hostage in an attempt to get into the security area.

398
00:31:35,700 --> 00:31:41,780
According to FBI reports, the security around American nuclear facilities is so successful

399
00:31:41,781 --> 00:31:46,200
that no hostile force has ever attempted to penetrate one.

400
00:31:46,201 --> 00:31:52,960
when we return to the high-tech and high-stakes world of military security.

401
00:31:56,920 --> 00:32:00,520
Special Forces units within the Department of Defense

402
00:32:00,521 --> 00:32:04,800
periodically test security at American nuclear facilities

403
00:32:04,801 --> 00:32:07,680
by attempting to penetrate their defenses.

404
00:32:09,280 --> 00:32:14,360
Security systems will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

405
00:32:16,200 --> 00:32:27,180
Inventor Lee Wheelbarger is a private citizen

406
00:32:27,181 --> 00:32:30,700
who believes that the best defense is an aggressive offense.

407
00:32:31,420 --> 00:32:35,580
Security has always depended on closely observing the bad guy.

408
00:32:36,160 --> 00:32:40,240
And Wheelbarger's electronic creations have extended the eyes and ears

409
00:32:40,241 --> 00:32:42,820
of America's intelligence network.

410
00:32:42,821 --> 00:32:47,780
What we have here is a pair of gyroscopically stabilized binoculars.

411
00:32:48,980 --> 00:32:51,180
Periodically, select reconnaissance teams

412
00:32:51,181 --> 00:32:53,800
trek to Wilbarger's rural Virginia retreat

413
00:32:53,801 --> 00:32:56,960
for an update on the latest in surveillance tools.

414
00:32:57,140 --> 00:32:58,840
In addition, that activates the binoculars.

415
00:32:59,800 --> 00:33:02,280
Anytime you depress on the top, you'll hear a click sound.

416
00:33:02,460 --> 00:33:05,120
As long as that's depressed, the image that you're seeing is stabilized.

417
00:33:05,740 --> 00:33:09,220
You just bring them up, and that gives you a stabilized image of the battlefield

418
00:33:09,221 --> 00:33:12,360
so that you can easily acquire targets and identify them,

419
00:33:12,361 --> 00:33:13,620
even from a moving vehicle.

420
00:33:14,620 --> 00:33:15,700
Go ahead and try it.

421
00:33:18,020 --> 00:33:21,680
Wheelbarger began his career by aiding local law enforcement.

422
00:33:22,360 --> 00:33:26,580
Policemen overcome by assailants are often held by their own handcuffs.

423
00:33:27,340 --> 00:33:30,400
Wheelbarger devised a universal handcuff key

424
00:33:30,401 --> 00:33:32,900
able to open any set of cuffs.

425
00:33:34,280 --> 00:33:36,220
High-profile kidnap targets

426
00:33:36,221 --> 00:33:38,800
from the late Princess Diana and her family

427
00:33:38,801 --> 00:33:40,360
to President Clinton

428
00:33:40,361 --> 00:33:44,080
have since worn the key hidden on their clothes or bodies.

429
00:33:47,560 --> 00:33:53,240
Wheelbarger specializes in deciphering the pictures created by thermal imaging cameras.

430
00:33:53,980 --> 00:33:56,700
When heat passes through the lens of such a camera,

431
00:33:56,701 --> 00:34:01,940
it strikes a detector array capable of registering minute temperature differences.

432
00:34:03,180 --> 00:34:07,080
The thermal pulse is then converted into a standard video image.

433
00:34:07,081 --> 00:34:12,800
You'll notice that we're observing an individual in this parking lot,

434
00:34:13,140 --> 00:34:17,100
but a small mouse is going to run across the lower part of the screen,

435
00:34:17,520 --> 00:34:19,440
and the thermal imager is going to pick it up.

436
00:34:20,120 --> 00:34:20,840
Here's our mouse.

437
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:22,620
As he comes up, runs across.

438
00:34:24,460 --> 00:34:26,460
That's how accurate a thermal imager is.

439
00:34:27,200 --> 00:34:33,140
Thermal imagers look through surfaces that would block even the most sensitive night vision equipment.

440
00:34:33,141 --> 00:34:38,420
Here is the heat from a boiler deep within a ship.

441
00:34:40,160 --> 00:34:45,880
These pictures also reveal how recently cars or any machinery have been operated.

442
00:34:49,060 --> 00:34:56,200
Now, the things that we can tell in this photograph is, number one, it was a cooler time of the year

443
00:34:56,201 --> 00:34:59,580
because you see a lot of hot right here right against the window.

444
00:34:59,880 --> 00:35:01,540
That's because the defroster was on.

445
00:35:01,541 --> 00:35:03,640
It heated the glass from the inside.

446
00:35:04,200 --> 00:35:07,500
We can also see that the rearview mirror has a heater inside of it.

447
00:35:07,880 --> 00:35:11,220
There's massive amounts of heat being radiated from underneath the vehicle.

448
00:35:11,500 --> 00:35:12,960
This is the muffler on the rear.

449
00:35:13,340 --> 00:35:16,000
This is the heat generated from underneath the engine compartment.

450
00:35:16,320 --> 00:35:19,420
And we can tell that the front brakes get hotter than the rear brakes.

451
00:35:19,660 --> 00:35:22,220
So obviously, we probably have disc brakes on the front.

452
00:35:23,920 --> 00:35:30,320
In some surveillance situations, miniaturization is the key to penetrating an enemy's defense.

453
00:35:31,540 --> 00:35:39,120
The camera is a high-resolution camera that can use a standard lens like we have on it here,

454
00:35:39,300 --> 00:35:47,640
or it can be fitted with a pinhole lens, which will allow us to look through a hole in the wall approximately the same size as a thumbtack makes.

455
00:35:48,000 --> 00:35:55,200
It gives us about 120 refilled of view inside the room, even though it's only looking through the hole the size of a thumbtack.

456
00:35:56,760 --> 00:36:00,120
Audio surveillance casts an even wider net.

457
00:36:00,121 --> 00:36:08,720
This handheld spectrum analyzer can listen in on any cell phone or wireless microphone transmission anywhere in the world.

458
00:36:10,360 --> 00:36:17,040
It tracks down signals in the RF spectrum and can detect up to 1,000 frequencies at a time.

459
00:36:17,960 --> 00:36:28,487
During the Gulf War, this device was used to monitor conversations between Saddam Hussein and his Iraqi ambassadors, providing information critical to planning the

460
00:36:28,488 --> 00:36:29,520
ground invasion.

461
00:36:30,120 --> 00:36:37,120
American forces in the Gulf also benefited from rifle-mounted infrared lasers.

462
00:36:37,121 --> 00:36:43,120
These beams tracked Iraqi troops at night without giving away Allied positions.

463
00:36:43,121 --> 00:36:51,120
In this demonstration, a container of warm water doubles as an enemy soldier.

464
00:36:51,121 --> 00:37:03,840
Laser beams are now so sensitive that they can hear distant conversations held behind closed doors,

465
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:08,760
converting tiny vibrations into audio recordings.

466
00:37:09,960 --> 00:37:15,860
What we have here is an infrared laser that will fire a beam up to three miles away.

467
00:37:16,600 --> 00:37:20,060
We shine this against the glass on the outside of a building,

468
00:37:20,061 --> 00:37:24,620
and we can measure the vibration of that glass with this.

469
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:28,680
As the glass vibrates from sounds inside the building,

470
00:37:28,960 --> 00:37:30,960
we can detect the movement of the glass

471
00:37:30,961 --> 00:37:34,860
and that allows us to hear what's taking place inside the building.

472
00:37:36,480 --> 00:37:40,340
Wheelbarger's innovations are highly sophisticated tools

473
00:37:40,341 --> 00:37:43,600
that can be used for good or ill.

474
00:37:44,920 --> 00:37:49,460
When we return, the technology that keeps powerful tools

475
00:37:49,461 --> 00:37:51,220
in the proper hands.

476
00:37:55,760 --> 00:37:58,440
The science of personal identification

477
00:37:58,441 --> 00:38:02,760
has long been critical to separating the good guys from the bad.

478
00:38:04,020 --> 00:38:07,160
Pin codes or passwords are often our only defense

479
00:38:07,161 --> 00:38:11,760
against the theft of our money, our secrets, and our very identity.

480
00:38:13,100 --> 00:38:16,720
But now, the new science of biometrics

481
00:38:16,721 --> 00:38:20,660
is poised to revolutionize personal identification.

482
00:38:21,860 --> 00:38:25,700
A combination of biology and physical measurements,

483
00:38:26,220 --> 00:38:30,440
biometrics builds on the century-old technique of fingerprinting.

484
00:38:32,260 --> 00:38:35,540
Three types of identification are in use today.

485
00:38:35,980 --> 00:38:38,540
One is what you know, pins or passwords.

486
00:38:39,120 --> 00:38:41,480
Another is what you have, your card.

487
00:38:42,340 --> 00:38:44,440
The third level is who you are.

488
00:38:45,200 --> 00:38:49,720
Fingerprint biometrics provides positive proof of who you are.

489
00:38:50,960 --> 00:38:57,180
This technology is already being combined with microcomputing to create safer, childproof guns.

490
00:38:58,240 --> 00:39:03,320
Smart guns are weapons that will only fire in the hands of an authorized user.

491
00:39:04,380 --> 00:39:12,320
When the gun is picked up, a fast fingerprint image sensor records an image that is immediately processed by a microchip.

492
00:39:12,321 --> 00:39:18,120
The fingerprint is then compared to an image stored in a memory chip.

493
00:39:19,320 --> 00:39:23,060
In less than a second, the weapon is either enabled or blocked.

494
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:35,660
At Chicago's O'Hare Airport, a test program uses fingerprint biometrics to increase the security of cargo shipments.

495
00:39:36,540 --> 00:39:42,160
Instead of the old pen and ink, a keypad electronically captures the prints of freight drivers.

496
00:39:42,320 --> 00:39:48,120
instantly confirming their identity and creating a detailed history for each shipment.

497
00:39:50,280 --> 00:39:55,880
The electronic print is faster than swiping an identity card or entering a PIN number,

498
00:39:56,200 --> 00:39:58,160
and more reliable.

499
00:40:03,600 --> 00:40:06,940
Fingerprint biometrics measures the density of a finger.

500
00:40:06,941 --> 00:40:12,860
If an especially ruthless assailant cut off the finger of an authorized person to gain access,

501
00:40:13,340 --> 00:40:17,160
the loss of blood would change the finger's density and render it useless.

502
00:40:19,920 --> 00:40:24,100
Of all the human physical characteristics that can be measured,

503
00:40:24,700 --> 00:40:27,520
none is more distinctive than the iris.

504
00:40:28,540 --> 00:40:34,600
Each iris pattern is absolutely unique and remains constant throughout a person's lifetime.

505
00:40:34,601 --> 00:40:43,380
Even exact twins who may have nearly identical DNA or fingerprints will have differences in their irises.

506
00:40:45,120 --> 00:40:50,880
In Texas, one bank chain has outfitted its ATMs with iris scanners.

507
00:40:51,860 --> 00:40:58,660
Like other biometrics, this system requires no ID card and no PIN number to remember.

508
00:40:58,661 --> 00:41:06,880
so imagine yourself walking up to the grocery store going to the checkout lane having no

509
00:41:06,881 --> 00:41:12,880
identification no driver's license no checkbook no credit card no debit card but merely walking

510
00:41:12,881 --> 00:41:18,580
up to a device that takes a picture of the iris of your eye identifies you can access your account

511
00:41:18,581 --> 00:41:22,260
and do the appropriate financial transaction debiting your account for the groceries that

512
00:41:22,261 --> 00:41:28,300
you've purchased and no id because it knows who you are the biometrics of facial recognition will

513
00:41:28,301 --> 00:41:33,200
soon allow your face to act as password and passport.

514
00:41:34,980 --> 00:41:40,900
The power of facial recognition is the discovery of a new algorithm of coding faces, which

515
00:41:40,901 --> 00:41:45,320
focuses on the region between the left temple, right temple, down below the tip of the nose.

516
00:41:45,600 --> 00:41:47,880
This region is known as the golden triangle of the face.

517
00:41:48,320 --> 00:41:52,840
Within this region, there are points on your face called the nodal points, and those are

518
00:41:52,841 --> 00:41:57,820
all independent of your expression, your aging, your tan, etc.

519
00:41:58,720 --> 00:42:04,080
Computers can currently analyze and compare 60 million faces a minute.

520
00:42:05,240 --> 00:42:10,540
The technology can operate at a distance and does not require a subject's cooperation,

521
00:42:11,280 --> 00:42:13,720
making it ideal for airport security.

522
00:42:14,940 --> 00:42:21,000
For international terrorists, airports will soon be very dangerous places to show their faces.

523
00:42:22,840 --> 00:42:31,080
In the London suburb of Newham, residents have enlisted facial recognition in their battle against crime.

524
00:42:32,040 --> 00:42:37,740
In 1998, 144 cameras were installed along the city streets,

525
00:42:38,480 --> 00:42:43,200
speeding pictures to a control center where a system called FACEIT

526
00:42:43,201 --> 00:42:48,180
continuously monitors every feed for 2,000 known criminals.

527
00:42:48,181 --> 00:42:58,260
The advantage is that I'm getting certain cameras monitored 365 days a year, 24 hours

528
00:42:58,261 --> 00:43:00,800
a day without the human element.

529
00:43:00,801 --> 00:43:05,360
The human element is then able to do other things, other more proactive work on other

530
00:43:05,361 --> 00:43:12,580
cameras, while the laborious slog of looking for certain faces in certain areas is automated.

531
00:43:12,581 --> 00:43:17,140
Since the program's introduction, there has been a 25% drop in crime,

532
00:43:17,380 --> 00:43:22,620
and at a lower cost than hiring dozens of additional police officers.

533
00:43:26,240 --> 00:43:32,560
In the Middle East, biometrics is helping a land of tradition face modern challenges.

534
00:43:34,380 --> 00:43:37,820
The State of Israel will adopt a biometric system

535
00:43:37,821 --> 00:43:43,100
to allow the workers who are traveling to Tel Aviv on a daily basis

536
00:43:43,101 --> 00:43:47,960
to enter without having to go through the laborious security checkpoints.

537
00:43:48,280 --> 00:43:49,980
They will check for facial characteristics.

538
00:43:50,680 --> 00:43:54,200
The system is a remarkable implementation of technology.

539
00:43:54,201 --> 00:44:00,120
It allows pretty much 40,000 workers to be entering a checkpoint in less than an hour.

540
00:44:01,240 --> 00:44:06,240
Mankind's dark side will always tempt some to steal what others have earned.

541
00:44:07,820 --> 00:44:18,320
Protectors and thieves are locked in a never-ending duel, a timeless battle where success or failure can hinge on the most modern technology.

